\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Spin
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In quantum physics Spin Value refers to the measured internal angular momentum of a subatomic particle (like an electron or photon) which behaves like a microscopic permanent magnetic needle that can only point in specific discrete directions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the specific context of the TU Delft 2015 dataset,
 "Spin Value" has a very practical meaning related to the physical setup of their experiment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Physical Meaning of Spin Value in the Delft Experiment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Delft team used nitrogen-vacancy (NV) center electron spins inside a diamond crystal lattice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Target:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

They trapped a single electron inside a microscopic defect in a diamond.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Measurement:
 
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

When they ran a trial,
 they applied a microwave pulse to read out the magnetic orientation of that trapped electron spin.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The Discrete Outcomes:
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 Under the laws of quantum mechanics,
 when you measure an electron's spin along an axis,
 it can never be "tilted" or partial.
 It must instantly snap to one of two discrete geometric configuration
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

• Spin-Up (often coded as +1 or binary 0)
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

• Spin-Down (often coded as -1 or binary 1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Why the Numbers CSV File Look Large
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You noticed that the raw outcomes in your CSV row look like massive integers (5424324 and 5672427).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this master layout file those aren't simple binary codes—
they are high-resolution raw photon counts emitted during the spin-readout phase.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To determine whether the electron was "Spin-Up" or "Spin-Down" the Delft hardware fires a green laser at the diamond after the microwave pulse:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If the electron is in the Spin-Up state it becomes optically active and floods the detector with thousands of fluorescent photons triggering a high count register (e.g.,
 5424324).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If the electron is in the Spin-Down state the system remains "dark" and registers significantly fewer counts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By setting a clear threshold splitting these high and low numbers the data scientists compress those large numbers into the sharp +1 and -1 variables that feed directly into your Kolmogorov-type joint expectation matrix.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Physics of the Condition (The Threshold Rule)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the 2015 Delft heralding data architecture,
 the spin-state readout isn't a pre-compiled binary code;
 it is a raw count value tracking how many fluorescent photon ticks hit the sensor during an assignment window.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Spin-Up (+1):
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The electron state is highly responsive to the optical laser probe,
 flooding the nanowire registers with raw counts (which is why your row shows massive integers like 5424324 or 5672427).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Spin-Down (-1):
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

 The electron remains dark or fails to cross the ionization gate,
 resulting either in exactly 0 or a very low background noise count.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because a successful herald row in this file means the signal was already physically verified any value greater than zero constitutes a valid fluorescent "Spin-Up" detection.
\end_layout

